  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nYesterday was soft across both locations\u2014Port Washington down 19%, Hicksville down 27%\u2014but this is primarily a capacity story, not a performance story. Port Washington ran 4 groomers and hit $554 per groomer-day, which is 20% above your trailing 30-day baseline; Hicksville ran 4 groomers at $384 per groomer-day, slightly below baseline. The real issue: transaction counts dropped 21% at Port Washington and 24% at Hicksville. This suggests appointment availability tightened mid-week as you staffed up for what should have been a strong Labor Day weekend\u2014except Labor Day itself (Monday) will depress revenue ~30%. You're seeing the booking cliff before the holiday, not a demand problem.\n\nMonth-to-date, you're actually outperforming. Port Washington is up 19% total, but DOW-adjusted expectation reveals you're 17% behind where the calendar mix should land\u2014a real performance gap that needs attention. Hicksville is exceptional: up 120% versus August's first three days, though DOW-adjusted you're 9% light. Both stores show the same pattern: add-on revenue divergence. Port Washington's attach rate collapsed from 54% to 37%; Hicksville's improved from 33% to 50%. This upsell discipline inconsistency between locations is operational\u2014flag your grooming leads on the gap.\n\nOne thing to feel good about: year-to-date, you're up 44% at Port Washington ($699k vs $486k last year), with groomer-days up from 3.3 to 5.3 per day. Capacity expansion is working. One thing to watch: Hicksville's $/groomer-day is $364 MTD versus $445 last month, and you have zero bather hours scheduled for the next 7 days. That's constraining your add-on potential.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Today you have 7 groomers and 15.0 bather hours (Port Washington leading); tomorrow 4 groomers and 8.0 bathers; next 7 days 39 groomer-days and 76.0 bather hours total, with Hicksville at zero bather support.**"
